diff --git a/.codex/skills/design-review/SKILL.md b/.codex/skills/design-review/SKILL.md
index a76cdb6..85649ea 100644
--- a/.codex/skills/design-review/SKILL.md
+++ b/.codex/skills/design-review/SKILL.md
@@ -15,6 +15,15 @@ This skill owns interface evaluation, design rationale and review follow-up. It
 
 ## Workflow
 
+For Windows/Fluent or named-IDE comparisons, use the
+[reference fidelity review](references/visual-quality.md#review-windows-or-ide-reference-fidelity)
+with the consumer's adopted product/version and declared exceptions.
+
+For hierarchy, typography, density and composition findings, use
+[visual quality](references/visual-quality.md). Compare the same task/content/state
+at the relevant sizes and themes; report visual inspection separately from
+keyboard, accessibility and behavioral observations.
+
 1. **Frame the task and decision.** State the user goal, completion condition, material risks and constraints. Distinguish the product outcome from the immediate review decision. Use existing requirements and decision authority; a small critique needs no full specification process.
 2. **Collect relevant evidence.** For an interface critique, trace entry, choices, submission, pending, empty, error, recovery and completion states as applicable. Record screen/state, action and result. Assess hierarchy, labels, feedback, navigation, consequences and consistency. Check keyboard/focus and relevant accessibility criteria when the interface permits; screenshots cannot establish them. For communication-only work, identify what the supplied evidence supports and what remains unknown.
 3. **Clarify and classify.** Connect each observation to task impact. Distinguish reproducible failure, plausible risk, preference and research hypothesis. When feedback proposes a control or layout, clarify the underlying need before accepting or rejecting the solution. Confirm material paraphrases; do not infer hidden motives or strengthen someone else's claim.
diff --git a/.codex/skills/design-system/SKILL.md b/.codex/skills/design-system/SKILL.md
index 0f88938..19efbad 100644
--- a/.codex/skills/design-system/SKILL.md
+++ b/.codex/skills/design-system/SKILL.md
@@ -1,6 +1,6 @@
 ---
 name: design-system
-description: Create or evolve shared UI tokens, components, variants, states, and usage contracts. Use when implementing reusable interfaces, resolving component inconsistency, or maintaining a design system. Do not use for unrelated backend work or a purely aesthetic opinion; use design-review for evidence-based journey evaluation.
+description: Create or evolve shared UI tokens, components, visual direction and screen layouts composed from a design system. Use for reusable interfaces, cross-screen composition, component inconsistency or system maintenance. Use design-review for journey evaluation; skip backend architecture and isolated cosmetic fixes.
 ---
 
 # Design System
@@ -15,6 +15,17 @@ This skill owns reusable interface contracts and their evolution. A one-off scre
 
 ## Workflow
 
+When the consumer adopts Windows/Fluent or a named IDE as its reference, apply
+[reference translation](references/visual-composition.md#translate-a-windows-or-ide-reference)
+to separate visual lineage, product decisions and toolkit capabilities.
+
+For shared visual direction or screen composition, use
+[visual composition](references/visual-composition.md) to connect task hierarchy,
+geometry, typography and semantic roles. For a concrete workbench, table, board,
+form, palette or conversation, consult only the applicable row in
+[interaction patterns](references/interaction-patterns.md). These methods extend
+the existing system; they do not require a new style, framework or system rebuild.
+
 1. **Locate the contract.** Find the actual component entry points, token definitions and consumers. Record purpose, content constraints, semantics, inputs/events, state transitions and compatibility obligations. Start from the requested change rather than rebuilding the system.
 2. **Compare reuse choices.** Reuse an existing pattern when purpose and behavior agree. Add a bounded variant when shared meaning remains stable. Separate components when divergent responsibilities produce interacting flags or incompatible semantics. Similar pixels alone are insufficient evidence for consolidation. Read [pattern decisions](references/pattern-decisions.md) for this choice.
 3. **Specify relevant states.** Cover default, hover, focus, selected, disabled, loading, empty, error and success as applicable. Define entry/exit conditions, recovery, content preservation and concurrent-action behavior. Explain unavailable actions when users need that information. Do not add irrelevant states mechanically.
